a three-day travel ban is in force in  portugal's capital lisbon  authorities are worried about the  growing number of cases of the  coronavirus delta variant  slowing down traffic in an attempt to  slow down the spread  of covert 19. police began checking  drivers entering or leaving lisbon on  friday afternoon  authorities announced the measures just  the day before but officers say most  residents knew  something was coming their way  despite the short notice drivers know  the rules  they're aware of the ban on leaving or  entering the lisbon metropolitan area  and we've seen much less traffic than  the usual friday afternoon  lisbon's 2.8 million residents can only  leave the area with a valid reason  and those living outside are barred from  entering  they're tough measures but most people  appear to accept them  i think it's really difficult to have an  opinion but i'm relatively confident  about what the government decided  i think things are being decided in the  face of the uncertainty of the situation  most people here are very aware of just  how dangerous the virus can be  portugal was hit hard earlier in the  year the country's health system was on  the verge of collapse  after a lockdown was imposed in january  the tide gradually turned  infection rates dropped and restrictions  were almost completely lifted  but less than a month after portugal  reopened its borders to tourists from  the uk  and the rest of the eu the country is  seeing a new surge  the government blamed the rapid spread  of the delta variant  for the sudden increase in cases  we're still waiting for more data more  official sequencing data  but the indicators are pointing towards  a greater dominance of this variant  this is why it's important to take this  decision  portugal's government says it's  monitoring the rising cases  and whether the snap travel ban will be  repeated  will be decided weekly  you
 

